SH.01 · Shared — Get human moves for a position ^LfBrYefZ

SH.02 · Shorten the position: drop the last two counters. This shortened form is both the lookup key and what gets sent to Lichess. ^MFlW5QdA

SH.03 · Does the local cache contain at least one saved item — either a move or a “no games here” marker — for each of the three sets? ^e2d0bpCP

All three. ^rRLPtBjP

Some missing. ^pQDuQNAc

SH.05 · If even one set has no saved item, do not use any cached moves for this call. Ask for all three in turn — masters, then elite, then amateur:
· ask Lichess (see note)
· use each successful answer for this call
· save each returned move to cache; older cached moves missing from the new answer are not deleted
· if the answer has no moves, save a “no games here” marker; older move entries are still not deleted
· if the request failed or was skipped, use an empty set for this call and save nothing
· wait 1 second before asking for the next set ^IHwEWMBY

SH.04 · Load the stored moves for all three sets from local cache. No network at all. Ignore every “no games here” marker. A set comes back empty only if it has no stored move alongside the marker. ^vpYvGpxV

What “ask Lichess” really does. Each round allows up to 10 attempts. If Lichess says “too many requests”, wait 2 seconds before the next attempt. If the connection fails or the reply cannot be read, wait 1 second before the next attempt. For any other unsuccessful response, give up on that set immediately. If the tenth attempt also ends in one of the retryable failures, the script pauses and asks you in the terminal: press Enter to begin another round, [n] to give up on this set, or [s] to stop the whole run. The request carries your Lichess token if one is set. ^wNdLT1pI

